  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Evaluating Behavior Change Approaches to Household Water Purification</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Globally, more than half a million children under five die from diarrhea each year, yet many of these deaths can be prevented by access to safe drinking water. Chlorine tablets are inexpensive and can be used to purify water, which drastically reduces diseases in countries with poor water, sanitation, and hygiene systems. Less than one percent of the population of Pakistan report using chlorine and only seven percent report using any water purification tools at all. Studies demonstrated that free chlorine distribution increased its use, but much of the population still does not treat their water. The implementing partner piloted two new promising behavior change approaches to increase households use of chlorine: learning and habit formation. The implementing partner will test and compare these approaches using a randomized controlled trial with four arms: a control group will only receive advice on the benefits of using chlorine tablets; a monitoring group will receive advice and free chlorine tablets; a learning group will receive advice, chlorine tablets, regular assistance from health workers to record diarrheal incidence, and information about the diarrhea rate in the control group as a social incentive; and a habit formation group will receive advice, tablets, and a small reward for appropriate daily usage of tablets. Lessons from this study will inform the implementing partners water, sanitation, and hygiene programs and have the potential to influence the governments network of 125,000 community health workers who reach nine million rural and urban households in informal settlements in Pakistan.</narrative>
  </description>
